    "result_text": "Build is green. Here's what I implemented for the `Idempotency-Key` behavior on `POST /api/owners`.\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a create repeats with an already-seen `Idempotency-Key`, the endpoint now returns the originally created owner with **200** instead of creating a duplicate (which would otherwise hit the household duplicate check and return 409).\n\n### How it works\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline gained a first step and a post-save step, following the codebase's existing conventions (small function-per-step classes wired in YAML, state via `Out<T>`/`@Val`, control-flow diversions via checked-exception escalations):\n\n1. **`CheckIdempotency`** (new first step) reads the header via Spring's `@RequestHeader(name = \"Idempotency-Key\", required = false)` (confirmed supported by the OfficeFloor Spring-MVC-argument integration). It publishes the key as `Out<String>`, then:\n   - no key / new key \u2192 continues to `build` as normal;\n   - already-seen key \u2192 loads the original owner, maps it to `OwnerDto` *while the transaction is still open*, and throws `IdempotentReplayException` to short-circuit the pipeline before the duplicate check.\n2. **`RecordIdempotency`** (new step after `save`, once the id is assigned) records `key \u2192 ownerId` in the store when a key was supplied.\n3. **`IdempotentReplayExceptionHandler`** (escalation) responds `200 OK` with the carried `OwnerDto`.\n\n### Files added\n- `rest/idempotency/IdempotencyStore.java` \u2014 `@Component`, a thread-safe `key \u2192 ownerId` map (in-memory, `putIfAbsent`).\n- `rest/function/owner/CheckIdempotency.java`, `RecordIdempotency.java`\n- `rest/escalation/IdempotentReplayException.java` (checked, carries the `OwnerDto`), `IdempotentReplayExceptionHandler.java`\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026IdempotentReplayException.yml`\n\n### Files changed\n- `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted `checkIdempotency` first and `recordIdempotency` after `save`.\n\n### Notes / design choices\n- I used the escalation mechanism to divert control (the only branching mechanism actually used in this codebase \u2014 no `@Flow` usage exists) rather than reading header binding into the responder. Mapping to the DTO happens inside the governed step so no lazy-loading occurs after the transaction closes.\n- The store is in-memory (retry-safe within an instance, not durable across restarts) \u2014 matching the spec's stated behavior; a note in the class documents that.\n- `coreIdempotentRepeat` passes and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\nOne thing worth flagging: the acceptance suite currently contains a single test. The change is consistent with the surrounding rules, but if you have additional idempotency edge cases in mind (e.g. same key with a *different* body, or key reuse across different resources), those aren't exercised yet \u2014 let me know if you'd like me to handle any of them.",
